# Cloudflare Pages / Netlify. Copy next to the contents of dist/client.
# Static files (sw.js, manifest.webmanifest, assets) win over this rule because redirects
# without a force flag (!) only apply when no file matches.
# A missing hashed asset (a stale tab asking for an old chunk) must be a 404, never the HTML
# shell, which the browser would try to run as JavaScript. Netlify applies the first matching
# rule, so this rule must stay before the shell rule. Netlify shows its own 404 page when the
# site has no 404.html. Cloudflare Pages does not support the 404 status in this file and ignores
# this line, so a missing asset on Cloudflare Pages still gets the shell.
/assets/*  /404.html  404
/*  /_shell.html  200
